{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Permissions{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <h1>Your permissions</h1>
    <p>
        Computed in code by the <code>AccessInspector</code> service, which calls
        <code>AccessControlManager::decide()</code> directly — no <code>#[AccessPolicy]</code>
        attribute involved. Each cell shows the verdict and the voter's reason.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>VIEW</th><th>EDIT</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for row in rows %}
                <tr>
                    <td>#{{ row.document.id }} · {{ row.document.title }} <span class="muted">({{ row.document.owner }})</span></td>
                    <td>{{ row.view.granted ? '✅' : '⛔' }} <span class="muted">{{ row.view.reason }}</span></td>
                    <td>{{ row.edit.granted ? '✅' : '⛔' }} <span class="muted">{{ row.edit.reason }}</span></td>
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted">
        The same verdicts back Twig's <code>is_granted('VIEW', document)</code> and Security's
        <code>isGranted()</code>: with the component enabled, both are bridged to this very
        decision manager.
    </p>

    <h2>Both Twig functions, one decision</h2>
    <p>
        Registering the bundle splits Security's Twig extension in two:
        <code>is_granted()</code> and <code>is_granted_for_user()</code> are <em>taken over</em> by
        the component, while <code>access_decision()</code> and the
        <code>impersonation_*</code> helpers stay <em>delegated</em> to Security
        (through <code>SecurityExtensionWithoutAuthorization</code>). They still agree, because
        Security's decision manager is itself the component's adapter — so the message below is
        written by <code>DocumentVoter</code>, reached through Security's own object.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th><code>is_granted('VIEW', doc)</code></th><th><code>access_decision('VIEW', doc)</code></th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for row in rows %}
                {% set decision = access_decision('VIEW', row.document) %}
                <tr>
                    <td>#{{ row.document.id }} · {{ row.document.title }}</td>
                    <td>{{ is_granted('VIEW', row.document) ? '✅ granted' : '⛔ denied' }}</td>
                    <td>
                        {{ decision.isGranted ? '✅ granted' : '⛔ denied' }}
                        <span class="muted">{{ decision.getMessage() }}</span>
                    </td>
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Two vocabularies, one verdict</h2>
    <p>
        <code>access_decision()</code> could not be taken over: it hands back an object of
        Security's, whose shape is not this component's — a boolean <code>isGranted</code> against
        a three-valued <code>decision</code>, <code>getMessage()</code> against <code>reason</code>.
        A function whose return type depended on which bundles are installed would be a trap, so the
        component publishes the same question under a name of its own,
        <code>access_control_decision()</code>. Both are available at once, which is what lets a
        template move one page at a time rather than in a single commit.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Document</th>
                <th><code>access_decision('VIEW', doc)</code><br><span class="muted">Security's shape</span></th>
                <th><code>access_control_decision('VIEW', doc)</code><br><span class="muted">the component's shape</span></th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for row in rows %}
                {% set legacy = access_decision('VIEW', row.document) %}
                {% set native = access_control_decision('VIEW', row.document) %}
                <tr>
                    <td>#{{ row.document.id }} · {{ row.document.title }}</td>
                    <td>
                        <code>.isGranted</code> = {{ legacy.isGranted ? 'true' : 'false' }}<br>
                        <code>.getMessage()</code> = <span class="muted">{{ legacy.getMessage() ?: '—' }}</span><br>
                        <code>.strategy</code> = <code>{{ legacy.strategy ?: '—' }}</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>
                        <code>.decision</code> = <code>{{ native.decision.value }}</code><br>
                        <code>.isGranted()</code> = {{ native.isGranted ? 'true' : 'false' }}<br>
                        <code>.reason</code> = <span class="muted">{{ native.reason ?: '—' }}</span>
                    </td>
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted">
        <code>.strategy</code> is worth a second look. Security fills it with the word the
        application wrote, and installing the bundle used to leave it empty — a template that
        displayed <em>affirmative</em> quietly displayed nothing. The bridge now reports the word
        back, and it is still Security's: this app names the very same algorithm
        <code>permit_overrides</code> under <code>access_control.default_strategy</code>, and a
        template written against Security keeps reading its own vocabulary.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
        The three-valued <code>decision</code> is the truth of the component's object;
        <code>isGranted()</code> only says whether access was granted, so an abstention answers
        <code>false</code> exactly as a refusal does.
    </p>

    {% if is_granted('ROLE_ADMIN') %}
        <p>
            <a href="{{ path('app_permissions_preview') }}">Access preview →</a> — decide for
            <em>other</em> accounts in a template with <code>is_granted_for_user()</code>.
        </p>
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}
